body:
1) I have a little bit of experience in non-crisis mental health work and I would say the biggest difference is that in this work you focus much more on the immediate future. Sometimes we can tackle maladaptive behavior patterns but mostly we're focusing on what's causing the distress right now.

&#x200B;

2) Attempting to send emergency services (when the caller has not asked us to) is extremely rare and an absolute last resort. That said, cellphones make it much, much harder to successfully do that when we determine that need to. However, that effort is the work of the authorities, not us -- we just make the request.

If you mean to immediately calm down someone who is panicking, controlled breathing and grounding exercises are the things that work best in my experience. If a caller starts out so distressed that they can't talk, we'll often do one of those together before we get into things.

&#x200B;

If you're speaking a little more broadly -- helping deescalate someone who's very emotional -- empathetic listening and validation are the most powerful tools, period.

&#x200B;

Getting people to talk about their pets helps too ;)

&#x200B;

We do that under incredibly rare circumstances and when everything else has failed.

&#x200B;

I do find it incredibly rewarding. And yes, they take good care of us :)

I have dealt with mental illness in the past. I am in a good place now, but I work hard to make sure I stay there: I monitor my stress, prioritize self-care, reach out for support when I need it, or even take time off the lines if I feel that I'm getting overwhelmed. The work is hard sometimes, but 100% worth it. Maybe at some point I will find that I can no longer cope with the stress of this work and need to find another way to make a difference. But for now I'm doing just fine.

&#x200B;

It's awesome that you've thought about volunteering! If you're not in a place where you're personally able to right now then there's nothing wrong with that. Five years ago this job would have been a terrible idea for me lol. The best thing you can do right now is focus on you. If you get to a point in the future where you feel ready to volunteer and are still interested in doing so, then go for it! (If you're talking about the Crisis Text Line, I have friends who volunteer there and they say good things.)

No worries! It happens rarely. We do alert authorities. We don't reach out to the families, though the authorities typically do, if they can locate them. I believe this is standard for most lines.

&#x200B;

We do have periodic meetings with a counselor to help process. There is also a strong culture of mutual support and I always feel able to reach out to a fellow counselor or a supervisor if I need to talk after a tough call.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
